<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Smyrna</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Smyrna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ancient Greek city on the Gulf of Izmir, a claimed birthplace of Homer, destroyed by Lydia and refounded in the Hellenistic age.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-smyrna">Old Smyrna</a>, at Bayraklı on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-izmir">Gulf of Izmir</a>, was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolians">Aeolian Greeks</a> c. 1000 BCE and taken over by Ionian exiles from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colophon">Colophon</a>, probably in the 8th century BCE. It developed into one of the first Greek cities with planned streets and stone walls, and claimed to be the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city prospered from the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Hermus</a> plain. Its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> is among the earliest monumental Ionian sanctuaries. It was not one of the original twelve members of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Ionian League</a>. Smyrna fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia</a>, resisting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia">Gyges</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a> destroyed it c. 600 BCE, after which it survived only as villages.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> refounded Smyrna on the slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pagus">Mount Pagus</a> c. 300 BCE, and it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Ionian League</a>. It sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> and passed into the province of Asia after 133 BCE, growing into the modern city of Izmir.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/smyrna.jpg" alt="Smyrna" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agora_of_Smyrna,_built_during_the_Hellenistic_era_at_the_base_of_Pagos_Hill_and_totally_rebuilt_under_Marcus_Aurelius_after_the_destructive_178_AD_earthquake,_Izmir,_Turkey_(18702047681).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Smyrna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Izmir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=smyrna" aria-label="Smyrna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5355 798 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Smyrna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5675 1054L5697 1052M5675 1054L5673 1031M5675 1054L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 1052h0M5673 1031h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5675 1054h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5675 1054h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=smyrna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

